rizontal work plus reading S*N initial + (N-S)*N reads = N^2 reads. So overall O(N^2). Good.\n\nB uses prefix sum table and OpenMP parallel over rows, also O(N^2), but stores full prefix table (n+1)^2 longs ~ 64M for n=8000? Actually (8001)^2*8 ≈ 512 MB, too large maybe. But measured mean 0.035s slower due to memory.\n\nC uses column-prefix sums (vertical) and OpenMP over rows with horizontal sliding; stores pref of size (n+1)*n ints ~ 64M bytes? Actually n=8000 -> 8001*8000*4 ≈ 256 MB, still large. But measured 0.01224.\n\nA is fastest among these three individually? A mean 0.0120148; C 0.0122413; B 0.0350688. So A and C similar. Our best own experiments around 0.0114-0.0115, slightly faster than A/C.\n\nWe need to integrate diverse archived transformations. We can combine:\n- Custom buffered scanner (A or C) - both have.\n- Ring buffer streaming column sums from A (memory efficient).\n- OpenMP parallelization? But ring buffer approach is inherently sequential due to reading rows sequentially and updating colSum; cannot easily parallelize across row positions because each step depends on previous colSum. However we could use prefix-sum approach with OpenMP as in C/B, but memory heavy. Maybe combine A's streaming with horizontal sliding per row (already) plus maybe vectorization? But no explicit SIMD.\n\nAlternative: Use column-prefix sums like C but avoid storing full matrix; store only vertical prefix sums of each column incrementally while reading rows? Actually to compute SxS windows, we need for each starting row i and col j the sum over s rows. We can maintain a sliding window vertically as we read rows: keep an array \"colSum\" of current S-row window (like A), then for each new row update colSum by subtracting old row and adding new row; but to compute all horizontal windows at that vertical position, we need the full column sums for that vertical window. That's exactly A's approach. It reads rows sequentially and computes max over horizontal positions per v