for the horizontal sliding within each vertical step to allow OpenMP across columns, but that adds overhead. Let's estimate: For each vertical step (N-S+1 ~ up to 8000), inner loop length N-s+1; if s small, say 1, then we have 8000 steps * 8000 windows = 64M operations; OpenMP with 4 threads could speed up but thread creation overhead per step would kill. Better to parallelize across vertical steps by storing all rows and using prefix sums (C). But C's time is similar to A despite OpenMP, because memory bandwidth dominates.\n\nGiven our best own experiments achieved ~0.0114 vs A 0.01201, a small improvement from unrolling might get there. Let's propose that.\n\nWe need to produce complete file content. We'll write code based on A but with:\n- Larger buffer (1<<20).\n- Use `int` for matrix values and `long long` for sums.\n- Unroll the inner loop in steps of 4 for reading/updating columns, handling remainder.\n- Maybe use `#pragma GCC ivdep` or `__restrict__`.\n\nLet's craft code carefully.\n\nWe must ensure correctness: The algorithm reads exactly N*N integers. For each new row step (1..n-s), we read one full row of n ints and update ring slot. After processing all steps, done. Note that after last step (step = n-s), we have processed rows up to index? Let's verify A's loop: for step=1; step<=n-s; ++step reads new row at position i+s where i starts 0? Actually initial top window uses rows 0..s-1. Then step=1 reads row s, updates colSum to represent rows 1..s (since old row 0 replaced), computes windows for starting row 1. So after loop ends when step=n-s, we have processed starting rows up to n-s? Let's check: initial top window covers i=0. Then each step increments i by 1 and reads new bottom row. After k steps, current vertical window starts at i=k (since old top removed). The last valid start is i = n-s. So loop should run for step from 1 to n-s inclusive? If step=n-s, then after that we have processed starting row n-s. Yes A uses `for (int step = 1; step <= n - s; ++step)`. Good.\n\nEdge cases: s could be 0? No, S>0 and S<N. N up to 7999 maybe. If s=1, ring size n*1 = n, works. The initial top window reads first row (s=1), computes horizontal max over all windows of width 1 (i.e., each element). Then for step=1..n-1, read next row and update colSum to that row's values, compute max. Works.\n\nNeed to handle reading when n-s could be large; loop fine.\n\nLet's write code with unrolling:\n\n```\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdint>\n\nstruct FastScanner {\n    static const int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\n    char buf[BUFSIZE];\n    size_t idx, len;\n    FastScanner() : idx(0), len(0) {}\n    inline char getChar() {\n        if (idx >= len) {\n            len = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, stdin);\n            idx = 0;\n            if (len == 0) return 0;\n        }\n        return buf[idx++];\n    }\n    int nextInt() {\n        char c;\n        do { c = getChar(); } while (c && c <= ' ');\n        int sign = 1;\n        if (c == '-') { sign = -1; c = getChar(); }\n        int x = 0;\n        while (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {\n            x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\n            c = getChar();\n        }\n        return sign * x;\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    FastScanner fs;\n    int n = fs.nextInt();\n    int s = fs.nextInt();\n\n    std::vector<long long> colSum(n, 0);\n    std::vector<int> ring((size_t)s * n);\n\n    // Read first S rows\n    for (int i = 0; i < s; ++i) {\n        int* row = &ring[(size_t)i * n];\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            int v = fs.nextInt();\n            row[j] = v;\n            colSum[j] += v;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Initial top window\n    long long cur = 0;\n    const long long* csInit = colSum.data();\n    for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += csInit[j];\n    long long mx = cur;\n    for (int j = s; j < n; ++j) {\n        cur += csInit[j] - csInit[j - s];\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n    }\n\n    int nextSlot = 0;\n    const int nMinusS = n - s;\n    for (int step = 1; step <= nMinusS; ++step) {\n        int* rowPtr = &ring[(size_t)nextSlot * n];\n        long long* cs = colSum.data();\n\n        // First S columns: update and build initial horizontal window\n        cur = 0;\n        int j = 0;\n        for (; j + 4 <= s; j += 4) {\n            int v0 = fs.nextInt();\n            int v1 = fs.nextInt();\n            int v2 = fs.nextInt();\n            int v3 = fs.nextInt();\n            long long d0 = (long long)v0 - rowPtr[j];\n            long long d1 = (long long)v1 - rowPtr[j+1];\n            long long d2 = (long long)v2 - rowPtr[j+2];\n            long long d3 = (long long)v3 - rowPtr[j+3];\n            rowPtr[j] = v0; rowPtr[j+1] = v1; rowPtr[j+2] = v2; rowPtr[j+3] = v3;\n            cs[j] += d0; cs[j+1] += d1; cs[j+2] += d2; cs[j+3] += d3;\n            cur += (cs[j] + cs[j+1] + cs[j+2] + cs[j+3]);\n        }\n        for (; j < s; ++j) {\n            int v = fs.nextInt();\n            long long delta = (long long)v - rowPtr[j];\n            rowPtr[j] = v;\n            cs[j] += delta;\n            cur += cs[j];\n        }\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n\n        // Remaining columns: update and slide horizontal window\n        for (; j + 4 <= n; j += 4) {\n            int v0 = fs.nextInt();\n            int v1 = fs.nextInt();\n            int v2 = fs.nextInt();\n            int v3 = fs.nextInt();\n            long long d0 = (long long)v0 - rowPtr[j];\n            long long d1 = (long long)v1 - rowPtr[j+1];\n            long long d2 = (long long)v2 - rowPtr[j+2];\n            long long d3 = (long long)v3 - rowPtr[j+3];\n            rowPtr[j] = v0; rowPtr[j+1] = v1; rowPtr[j+2] = v2; rowPtr[j+3] = v3;\n            cs[j] += d0; cs[j+1] += d1; cs[j+2] += d2; cs[j+3] += d3;\n            cur += (cs[j] - cs[j-s]) + (cs[j+1] - cs[j+1-s]) + (cs[j+2] - cs[j+2-s]) + (cs[j+3] - cs[j+3-s]);\n            if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n        }\n        for (; j < n; ++j) {\n            int v = fs.nextInt();\n            long long delta =