dles rest.\n\nAlso note: The initial top window reading uses simple loops without unrolling; could also unroll but not necessary.\n\nWe should include <cstdint> and maybe <algorithm>? Not needed.\n\nLet's test mentally with small example from statement:\nN=5 S=3\nMatrix rows as given.\nInitial read first 3 rows, colSum = [10,19,9,23,7] (sum row0+row1+row2: 3+5+2=10; 6+6+7=19; 7+2+0=9; 5+9+9=23; 3+1+3=7)\ncur = colSum[0..2]=38, mx=38.\nSlide j=3: cur += cs[3]-cs[0] = 23-10 =>51; mx=51. j=4: cur+=cs[4]-cs[1]=7-19=>39; no change.\nThen step=1 (n-s=2): nextSlot=0, rowPtr points to ring slot 0 which is old row0 [3,6,7,5,3]. Read new row index 3: values 6,0,6,2,6. Update:\nj=0: v=6 delta=3 cs[0]=13 cur+=13\nj=1: v=0 delta=-6 cs[1]=13 cur+=13 =>26\nj=2: v=6 delta=-1 cs[2]=8 cur+=8=>34 (cur now sum cs[0..2] = 13+13+8=34)\nmx remains 51.\nThen remaining j=3: read v=2 delta=-3 cs[3]=20; cur += cs[3]-cs[0] =20-13 =>41; mx still 51. j=4: read v=6 delta=3 cs[4]=10; cur+=cs[4]-cs[1]=10-13=>38.\nThen step=2 (n-s=2): nextSlot becomes 1, rowPtr points to old row1 [5,6,2,9,1]. Read new row index 4: values 1,8,7,9,2. Update:\nj0 v=1 delta=-4 cs[0]=9 cur+=9\nj1 v=8 delta=2 cs[1]=15 cur+=15=>24\nj2 v=7 delta=5 cs[2]=13 cur+=13=>37 (sum first 3 = 9+15+13=37)\nremaining j3: v=9 delta=0 cs[3]=20; cur += cs[3]-cs[0] =20-9 =>48; mx still 51. j4: v=2 delta=1 cs[4]=11; cur+=cs[4]-cs[1]=11-15=>44.\nFinal mx=51 correct.\n\nGood.\n\nNow, should we include OpenMP from B? Could add `#pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mx)` over the initial top window? Not needed. Or use OpenMP to parallelize across vertical steps by storing all rows? That would change algorithm significantly and likely slower due memory. Since A is fastest, keep sequential.\n\nBut integration requires diverse transformations; we can mention in notes that we retained A's streaming ring buffer and scanner, C's larger input buffer and unrolled loops (though C didn't have unroll), and B's idea of using 64-bit sums? Actually B used l